    "created_at": "2021-07-26T17:41:54Z",
    "updated_at": "2021-07-26T17:45:55Z",
    "body": "> because replacing the `~/` with the truncation symbol feels redundant here.\r\n\r\nIt's not replacing. `~/` doesn't show up at all and `%/` is prepended to the front. That's it.\r\n\r\n> It doesn't bring much value truncating the root here.\r\n\r\nThen set `truncation_length` to `4` and you get what you want.\r\n\r\n> For me it would make more sense if the truncatable path would start from the first directory.\r\n> So again it would look like this: `C:/This/Is` -> `C:/This/Is/Deep` -> `%/Is/Deep/Wow`\r\n\r\nNumber of shown parts in your example: 3 -> 4 (wrong) -> 3\r\n\r\nAgain, said in my first reply: this looks like a misunderstanding of `truncation_length`. Please use something like `🤔` instead of `%/` so it doesn't *look* wrong.",
